(1) Let $\xi \colon \mathbf{X} \rightarrow \mathbf{Y}$ be an isomorphism in $\mathcal{D} _{sg}$.
 Note  $\xi $ can be written as a left fraction
\[
\xymatrix{
\
&\mathbf{Z}
\ar@{->}[dl]_{u}
\ar@{->}[dr]^{f}
 \\
\mathbf{X}
\ar@{->}[rr]_{\xi = fu^{-1}}
&\
&\mathbf{Y}
}
\]
We note that $f$ is also an isomorphism in $\mathcal{D} _{sg}$. We have $\operatorname{cone}(u), \operatorname{cone}(f) \in \operatorname{thick}(A)$.

1(a) Say $\operatorname{cone}(u)$ is isomorphic to a bounded  complex $\mathbf{P}$ of free $A$-modules. Say $\mathbf{P}_j = 0$ for $j \geq m$. Then for any finitely generated $A$-module we have   $H_i(\operatorname{cone}(u)\otimes W) = 0$ for $i \geq m$. So  for all $n \geq 0$ we have $H_i(\operatorname{cone}(u) \otimes N/I^{n+1}N) = 0$ for $i \geq m$.
So by \ref{triangle}(1) we get $H_i(\mathbf{Z} \otimes N/I^{n+1}N) \cong H_i(\mathbf{X} \otimes N/I^{n+1}N)$ for all $ i \geq m$ and for all $n \geq 0$.
It follows that $\alpha_{I, N}(\mathbf{Z}) = \alpha_{I, N}(\mathbf{X})$. Similarly we get $\alpha_{I, N}(\mathbf{Z}) = \alpha_{I, N}(\mathbf{Y})$. The result follows.

1(b) This is similar to (a).

2 We note that $s$ is isomorphic to the image in $\mathcal{D} _{sg}$ of a triangle $t \colon  \mathbf{X}^\prime \rightarrow \mathbf{Y}^\prime \rightarrow \mathbf{Z}^\prime \rightarrow \mathbf{X}^\prime[-1]$ in  $\mathcal{K} ^{-,b}(\operatorname{proj} A)$,
see \cite[p.\ 97]{N}.

(a) This follows from \ref{triangle}(3) and 1(a) above.

(b) This follows from \ref{triangle}(6) and 1(b) above.
